WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.016
Designing an Arts-Integrated GenAI Lesson.

2
00:00:04.016 --> 00:00:08.989
GENAI 101 - Module 3: Bringing It to Your Classroom.

3
00:00:08.989 --> 00:00:14.727
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:15.327 --> 00:00:17.313
You've made eight artifacts.

5
00:00:17.313 --> 00:00:23.625
The final project asks you to turn one of them into a lesson
your future students can do.

6
00:00:23.625 --> 00:00:35.753
In this video I'll walk through the template using one
worked example: adapting the beat-making lesson for a
seventh-grade general music class, fifty minutes, one
session.

7
00:00:35.753 --> 00:00:38.803
Follow along with the template on the page.

8
00:00:39.403 --> 00:00:41.168
Start with the basics.

9
00:00:41.168 --> 00:00:47.428
A title in student language, the grade band, the class type,
and the duration.

10
00:00:47.428 --> 00:01:07.814
Then two standards: one arts standard, here the National
Core Arts Standards for creating music, and one technology
or AI literacy standard, here the ISTE computational thinker
standard, which lines up with the course's computational
thinking competency.

11
00:01:07.814 --> 00:01:10.222
The arts standard comes first.

12
00:01:10.222 --> 00:01:13.272
The tool is the medium, not the point.

13
00:01:13.872 --> 00:01:21.772
Write two or three objectives with observable verbs, and
label each as an art outcome or a GenAI competency.

14
00:01:21.772 --> 00:01:32.233
For our example: students will build a four-bar drum pattern
on a sixteen-cell grid and extend it to sixteen bars with at
least two variations.

15
00:01:32.233 --> 00:01:33.916
That's the art outcome.

16
00:01:33.916 --> 00:01:41.670
And: students will describe what an AI loop recommendation
decided and state whether they kept it and why.

17
00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.279
That's the competency.

18
00:01:43.279 --> 00:01:48.034
Notice that neither objective says 'students will use
Soundtrap'.

19
00:01:48.034 --> 00:01:50.960
Objectives describe learning, not tools.

20
00:01:51.560 --> 00:02:00.099
The hook surfaces the art concept before the tool appears:
the class claps the kick, snare, and hi-hat pattern by hand.

21
00:02:00.099 --> 00:02:06.055
Direct instruction puts the grid on the board and walks
through one worked example.

22
00:02:06.055 --> 00:02:14.307
Guided practice builds the four-bar pattern together, then
changes one cell at a time and listens to what happened.

23
00:02:14.307 --> 00:02:22.128
That's the one-variable cycle from the course, and it's
where students learn what the grid actually controls.

24
00:02:22.128 --> 00:02:27.510
Give them a small evaluation grid, three criteria, to judge
their own loop.

25
00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.304
Never accept 'I like it'.

26
00:02:29.904 --> 00:02:38.828
Independent creation is where students extend to sixteen
bars, add two variations, and try the loop recommendation
feature.

27
00:02:38.828 --> 00:02:50.001
The human-only step, the part the tool cannot do, is the
written list of variations and the sentence about what the
AI suggested and whether they kept it.

28
00:02:50.001 --> 00:02:55.298
Your rubric must assess that step; otherwise you're grading
the software.

29
00:02:55.298 --> 00:02:58.926
Share with a pair swap and two reflection prompts.

30
00:02:58.926 --> 00:03:01.973
Keep the rubric to three or four criteria.

31
00:03:02.573 --> 00:03:04.554
Three sections people skip.

32
00:03:04.554 --> 00:03:13.355
The ethics moment: two minutes during guided practice asking
where the loops in the library come from and who made them.

33
00:03:13.355 --> 00:03:24.357
The low-tech path: a paper grid and body percussion, with
one shared device per group, so the lesson survives a
blocked network or a tool that's down.

34
00:03:24.357 --> 00:03:32.058
Differentiation: a pre-filled grid for emerging learners and
a second instrument layer for advanced ones.

35
00:03:32.058 --> 00:03:35.946
And the AI credit line students attach to their work.

36
00:03:35.946 --> 00:03:40.567
Draft your plan, post it for peer feedback, revise, and
submit.

37
00:03:40.567 --> 00:03:46.141
This is the artifact that will travel with you into your
teaching portfolio.
